## Before every commit

1. Run `just ci` and fix what fails. Never weaken a check to get it passing: no lowered lint
   level, no new exception without a reason that holds up, no skipped or deleted test.
2. Spawn a fresh-context review subagent. Give it the diff (`git diff --cached`, or
   `git diff main...HEAD` for a branch), the task it implements, [docs/style.md](docs/style.md)
   and the [Google Rust Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/rustguide.html), and
   none of your own reasoning. Ask it to check the diff against the review checklist in
   `docs/style.md` and report findings with file and line.
3. Fix every finding that holds up, rerun `just ci`, and repeat the review if the fixes were
   more than mechanical.
4. Commit with a [conventional commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) message, one
   coherent change per commit.
5. Push to a branch and open a pull request for the user. Never push to `main`, merge a pull
   request, or change the repository's settings; the user does those.

## Silencing a lint

Put the exception on the smallest item it applies to, with a reason:

- a clippy or rustc lint: `#[expect(clippy::lint_name, reason = "...")]`;
- a custom lint: `#[cfg_attr(dylint_lib = "trellis_lints", expect(lint_name, reason = "..."))]`.
  The `cfg_attr` keeps ordinary builds, which don't know the lint, from warning about it.

That is the only way to silence a lint, and these all fail `just dylint`:

- `allow` and `warn`, and every inner lint attribute (`#![...]`), at the top of a crate or module
  or inside a function;
- an `expect` on a `mod` line, or on an `impl` block or trait for a lint that doesn't report at
  the block;
- any other `cfg_attr` around an `allow`, `warn` or `expect`, and a lint attribute built from
  macro variables or assembled by a macro;
- a lint attribute or `#[ignore]` that a macro writes and that would fail written by hand, unless
  `trusted_macro_crates` in `dylint.toml` lists the crate that defines the macro;
- an `expect` whose reason has fewer than 20 characters or fewer than 4 distinct words besides
  the words of the lint's name;
- an `expect` of a lint group, such as `warnings` or `clippy::pedantic`;
- any `#[clippy::...]` attribute, such as `#[clippy::msrv]`.

An `expect` also fails once its lint stops firing. Some rules have no exceptions at all:
`code_comment`, `suppression_policy`, `missing_workspace_lints`, `disabled_code`,
`needless_raw_identifier`, `file_named_after_item` and `non_leaf_module_items` (rename or move the
code instead), and `macro_definition` (a macro goes only in a crate `macro_crates` in `dylint.toml`
lists).

If a rule seems wrong for this project, say so to the user rather than changing it. A rule
changes only when the user asks, in a pull request of its own.

## Where a rule belongs

The repository is the next agent's memory: whatever pattern is in it gets copied, a workaround
included. So a rule lives as far up this list as it can, strongest first:

1. **Structure.** Types, crate boundaries and APIs that make the wrong thing impossible to write.
2. **Checks.** A lint level, a cargo-deny rule, a structure rule in `[workspace.metadata.policy]`,
   or a test.
3. **This file.** Guidance for what no check can decide, such as product boundaries.
4. **Review.** The checklist in [docs/style.md](docs/style.md).

When a review finding comes up a second time, move it up. A new custom lint or structure check
belongs in [trellis](https://github.com/265866/trellis), so every project gets it; ask the user
to add it there. A pull request can't change rules and code together. When a new rule flags
existing code, fix the code first, or land the rule with an `expect` carrying a real reason at
each place it flags. [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) has the order for that and for a new proc
macro.

## Comments

Rust code has no `//` or `/* */` comments except `// SAFETY:` ones, and the `code_comment` lint
enforces it. A comment drifts from the code, and it is where a workaround gets justified instead of
fixed: the next agent that reads it copies the workaround to the next place that looks alike,
rather than finding the best fix there. Before you reuse a workaround you find, even one an
`expect` reason explains, check that the problem it works around still exists. Put the why where
it is checked or reviewed with the code: a doc comment on the item, the name of a function or test,
an assertion message, or the `reason` of an `#[expect(...)]`. Configuration files (TOML, YAML, the
justfile) keep their comments, since they have nowhere else to say why.

## Code

- **Errors.** A failure that a caller can recover from returns a `Result` whose error says what
  failed and with what input. Never panic on user input, I/O or anything over the network; a
  panic is for a bug or a broken invariant.
- **Fail closed.** Input the code doesn't recognize is an error, not a default.
- **Logs.** No secret, token or personal data in a log line or an error message.
- **Tests.** A test of timeouts, expiry, ordering or retries uses a clock it controls or explicit
  timestamps, never a sleep. Fixtures hold made-up data, never real names, emails, addresses,
  keys or tokens.

## New crates

Each crate has one job. `just policy` checks every crate against these rules:

- **Place and manifest.** A crate goes under `crates/`, whose crates are the workspace's only
  members. It has no build script unless `build_script_crates` in `[workspace.metadata.policy]`
  lists it. Its `Cargo.toml` holds only the keys the checker allows, so tests can't be switched
  off there; its `[lints]` table is exactly `workspace = true`; and it inherits `edition`,
  `rust-version` and `publish` from the workspace.
- **Sources.** Its sources live under its own directory: every `#[path]` names a `.rs` file
  inside the crate, and there are no symbolic links.
- **Internal dependencies.** `internal_dependencies` in `[workspace.metadata.policy]` maps each
  crate to the workspace crates it may depend on directly. A crate missing from the map, or an
  edge the map doesn't list, fails, so a new crate or a new edge is a rule change. A crate in
  `dev_only_crates` may be a dev-dependency of any crate, and nothing else.
- **Proc macros.** A crate depends directly on a proc-macro crate only if `proc_macro_crates`
  lists it, since a proc macro writes code the custom lints don't check. A crate that re-exports
  a proc macro, such as `serde` with `derive`, isn't caught by that list; instead, `just dylint`
  fails on a lint attribute its macros write, unless `trusted_macro_crates` in `dylint.toml`
  lists the crate that defines the macro (`serde_derive`, not `serde`), and a lint level it writes
  over your code names only lints `trusted_macro_lints` lists for that crate.
- **Releases.** List the crate in `changelog_include` in `release-plz.toml`, in the rule change
  that allows it, so its commits reach the release notes; `just policy` fails until you do.
- **Configuration.** Tool configuration files such as `clippy.toml`, `rustfmt.toml` or
  `.cargo/config.toml` live only at the root, under their exact names.
- **Agent instructions.** This file is the only one for coding agents: any other `AGENTS.md` or
  `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.cursor/`, `.claude/` or
  `.github/copilot-instructions.md` fails.

